However, if you want the code in TypeScript for a class that represents the sub agent configuration, here's how you can define it:

```typescript
// sub_agent_config.ts
interface AutocorrectConfig {
  enabled: boolean;
  delay_autocorrect?: number;
}

interface SubAgentConfig {
  autocorrect: AutocorrectConfig;
  otherConfig?: any;
}

class SubAgentConfiguration {
  private config: SubAgentConfig;

  constructor(config: SubAgentConfig) {
    this.config = config;
  }

  public getAutocorrectConfig(): AutocorrectConfig {
    return this.config.autocorrect;
  }

  public setAutocorrectConfig(autocorrectConfig: AutocorrectConfig): void {
    this.config.autocorrect = autocorrectConfig;
  }

  public getOtherConfig(): any {
    return this.config.otherConfig;
  }

  public setOtherConfig(otherConfig: any): void {
    this.config.otherConfig = otherConfig;
  }
}

// Example usage:
const config: SubAgentConfig = {
  autocorrect: {
    enabled: true,
    delay_autocorrect: 500
  },
  otherConfig: {}
};

const subAgentConfig = new SubAgentConfiguration(config);
console.log(subAgentConfig.getAutocorrectConfig());
```

Note: In the above TypeScript code, we define an interface `SubAgentConfig` to represent the configuration of the sub agent. The `AutocorrectConfig` interface represents the autocorrect configuration, which includes a `delay_autocorrect` property. The `SubAgentConfiguration` class encapsulates the configuration and provides methods to get and set the autocorrect configuration.